          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The sword primarily signifies the Word of God as a penetrating spiritual weapon and instrument of judgment, while also contextually signifying divine judgment, war or violence, and governmental punitive authority.\",\"book_summary\":\"The sword signifies the Word of God wielded by the Spirit for discernment and judgment, together with the related senses of war, governing force, and guarded exclusion; in its war sense it is opposed to peace.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Sword = the Word of God as penetrating and judging divine speech, with established contextual senses of judgment, war, and civil authority.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Sword = the Spirit-wielded Word of God that discerns and executes judgment, and contextually the force of war or government carrying judgment out.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement is evident. The book selects and unifies several senses already identified by the consensus. It omits other recognized contextual uses such as division, sorrow, and martyrdom, but does not explicitly deny them.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book neither rejects the Word-of-God identification nor replaces it with a different referent; its additional war, judgment, governmental-authority, and guarding applications are all represented in the consensus.\",\"rationale\":\"The central identification is the same: the sword is chiefly the Word of God, especially as penetrating, discerning, and judicial speech. The book's further applications—Christ's mouth-sword, war that removes peace, governmental execution of wrath, and the sword guarding Eden—correspond directly to consensus meanings and cited examples. Describing peace as the opposite of the sword specifically in its war sense is a conventional contrast and does not alter the symbolic core.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The book may blur distinct contextual meanings by presenting divine speech, war, governmental coercion, and guarded exclusion as one integrated symbol rather than as separate uses. Even so, every component is recognized by the consensus, so this is organizational synthesis rather than a materially different identification.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
